<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagpur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagpur is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, near the geographical centre of the country. Founded around 1702 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakht-buland-shah">Bakht Buland Shah</a> of the Gond kingdom of Deogarh, it became from the 1740s the capital of the Bhonsle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, whose kingdom stretched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. The British annexed it in 1853, and it later served as capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a>; it is known for its oranges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagpur.jpg" alt="Nagpur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kawade Sahil · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deekshabhoomi_Nagpur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Chhatrapati at Satara to the Peshwa's administration at Pune, which supervised a confederacy of semi-autonomous chiefs, among them the Gaekwads of Baroda, Holkars of Indore, Scindias of Gwalior and Bhonsles of <b>Nagpur</b>, who collected revenue and maintained armies while acknowledging the Peshwa's leadership and the Chhatrapati's formal sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…in the 19th century; in 1956 B. R. Ambedkar, the jurist and campaigner for the rights of the Dalits, became a Buddhist at <b>Nagpur</b> with hundreds of thousands of followers; and in the 20th century Buddhist teachers and Asian migrants established communities in Europe, North America and Australia.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Mahurjhari, near <b>Nagpur</b>, became a major centre of bead manufacturing in the early historic period.</blockquote><a href="/culture/south-indian-megalithic-culture">South Indian megalithic culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Savarkar, an atheist, opposed the caste system: under him the Hindu Mahasabha held all-caste dinners in <b>Nagpur</b> and Kanpur, and he gave parents money so that their children of the so-called lower castes would attend school.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century the Bhonsle Marathas of <b>Nagpur</b> extended their power over the region, and Chanda was annexed around 1751.</blockquote><a href="/state/chanda-kingdom">Chanda Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>He founded the city of <b>Nagpur</b> around 1702, which became the capital under his son Chand Sultan.</blockquote><a href="/state/deogarh-kingdom">Deogarh Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
